Now we're going to do the mushrooms.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:55.450
Now, this is the way you always saute mushrooms if they're to be sauteed,

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:59.590
but first I want to show you about washing them because that's important, too.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:06.780
Now, we've got these. Here are two mushrooms. These are both fresh ones, but this one is a

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:12.430
little bit fresher than that one, and you can tell because the stem is attached to the cap like that.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:19.120
This one is still fresh. If it were not fresh at all, the cap would have spread way out, but it still

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:25.600
has that nice curl so you know it is fresh. And you want to trim a little bit of the stem off if

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.970
If necessary, most American mushrooms don't have any dirt on the end of it.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.600
These are nice and clean, but you always want to wash them because there may be a little sand.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:41.460
And if the cap and stem are open this way, you always want to take the stem out like that.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:43.800
With this one, you don't have to.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:46.650
Then you wash them.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.450
You dump them in the water like that.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.610
I'll move this over here.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:59.160
And you lift them up with your hands, and any sand that's in them will drop down to the very bottom.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.170
Then, when they're all washed, you drain them by lifting them like that.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:09.550
If you took the water and poured the mushrooms into the sieve,

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.110
of course, all the sand would fall back on them again.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:17.600
And then, before you do anything more about the mushrooms,

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.240
and particularly if you're going to saute them,

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:21.660
they've got to be absolutely dry.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:26.750
So you take a nice, clean towel, and you dry them off.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.760
Because if they're wet, they're not going to brown.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:38.370
What with electric washing machines and everything, you don't have to be upset about using towels for things like this.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:44.560
And now for cutting them, we in this stew, we don't want sliced mushrooms, we want quartered mushrooms.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.090
If they're sliced, they disappear a little bit too much.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.540
So to quarter them, you just take the cap and do like that.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:58.230
And then we want the stem to look like the cap so that we just cut it on the bias like that.

00:15:58.490 --> 00:15:59.530
There, I'll do another one.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.830
You'll remember, just like that.

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.830
If you have a great big mushroom, you might want to make three cuts like that,

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:12.630
so they'll all be about the same size, and then your dam is cut on the bias.

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:17.520
There, now our mushrooms are all ready, and we're going to saute them.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.920
I'm going to use, oh, wait a minute, I'll get rid of this.

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:27.280
I'm going to use one of these patent no-stick pans.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.900
I like them very much, because nothing sticks.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:36.250
some just as they say and i use it like an ordinary pan with oil in it and butter and

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:42.490
these are mushrooms as to what they they always say sauteed in butter but they aren't actually

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:48.570
sauteed in butter they're sauteed in butter and oil because if you use just butter your

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:54.610
butter's going to burn and the mushrooms will taste awful because burned butter doesn't taste well

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:59.750
so the oil fortifies the butter a little bit in other words

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.760
is able to heat up a little hotter.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:06.300
Now, we have to wait until the butter is hot enough,

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.200
and you can always tell how hot it is

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:09.700
by looking at the butter foam.

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:12.370
That's a very easy way of telling.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.130
And when it's foaming up like this,

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:16.630
it means that it isn't hot enough,

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:19.270
and as soon as the foam begins to subside,

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:21.670
the butter is hot, and you can then saute.

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.140
So it always takes a little while.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:26.900
You just have to be patient and wait.

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:29.050
But you can look at that butter foam now.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.910
See, it's still foaming, so you just still have to wait.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:41.060
But if you try to sauté in something in butter that isn't hot enough,

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:43.380
it just isn't there, it just aren't going to brown.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:46.260
So you have to be a little bit patient if you want success.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:49.940
Now that butter's hot enough, the foam has gone down,

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:51.720
so in go the mushrooms.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.620
I think I'll have room for them all.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:55.660
Yep, I do.

